# The three-valued X < Y of intervals with 2-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface",
# "Comparisons"): yes when every member of X is below every member of Y, x_u < y_l; no when none is,
# x_l >= y_u; both false (unknown) otherwise and when an operand is empty. By the comparisons of two
# limbs, exact on canonical values, and the leading limbs for the test of emptiness. Exact: unknown
# exactly when neither holds. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_less_2
input:         xl = xl0 xl1
input:         xu = xu0 xu1
input:         yl = yl0 yl1
input:         yu = yu0 yu1
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      interval yl yu
ensures:       trit yes no lt x y x=xl,xu y=yl,yu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the bounds compared, and a test of emptiness (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical operand

nx  = lt(xl0, const inf)
ny  = lt(yl0, const inf)
ne  = and(nx, ny)
a   = lt_2_2(xu0, xu1, yl0, yl1)
c   = le_2_2(yu0, yu1, xl0, xl1)
yes = and(a, ne)
no  = and(c, ne)
